  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tonight: One more warm to hot day, before a pattern \nchange. A broad trough sits off the Pacific Coast and a mild \nsoutherly flow is found in advance of it. In that flow some \nshortwave energy begins to eject northward. A more notable wave \ncomes around the east third of WA and ID this afternoon and \nevening, with deepening moisture (PWATS 130-150% of normal). It \nalso encounters modestly steep lapse rates. The previous forecast \nand the NBM have not shown much precipitation potential during the\nday, but most of the CAM models are indicating some showers \ndeveloping with it and the NBM has been under-doing this kind of \nthing much of the summer. So I increased PoPs for this afternoon \nand evening over the east third of WA and ID. I do not think it \nwill be very significant amounts, but some showers are definitely \npossible. I do not have enough confidence for t-storms but that is\nnot out of the question; the main potential is found over SE\nWA/lower ID this afternoon and some may slip northward tonight. \nSome breezy conditions develop this afternoon as well, with gusts \nof 15-20 mph. Temperature push into the 80s this afternoon.\n\nWednesday and Thursday: The offshore low spirals inland. This \nwill bring periods of rain to the Inland NW as shortwave pivot\naround it. The first round is forecast to increase through the \nday Wednesday and a second Wednesday night into Thursday AM. The \nhighest precipitation potential and amounts are forecast around \nthe southeast WA and mountains zones, with around 0.25 to 0.50 \ninches through Thursday night, locally higher. Some embedded \nt-storms are also forecast, but they are not expected to be very \nstrong at this time. We will have to keep an eye on any burn\nscars that may see rain for possible flooding/debris flows, but\nconfidence is low. \n\nWinds will be breezy through the period, with the strongest winds\non Wednesday out of the south to southwest gusting between 20 to \n40 mph. The strongest winds are forecast over central WA. Central \nWA will have less rain than other areas, so we will have to watch \nfor some patchy blowing dust. Gusty winds are forecast for \nThursday as well, with gusts around 15-30 mph. Temperatures will \nbe notably cooler, with highs in the mid-60s to upper 70s \nWednesday and 60s to low 70s Saturday. Overnight lows will start \nto drop in the 30s and 40s in spots. We will have to keep an eye \ntoward possible frost in some areas over eastern WA and ID, in the\nsheltered valley and outlying areas, Thursday night into Friday \nAM.\n\nFriday to Monday: The Inland NW remains dominated by low pressure, as\nmodels trend to the low being cut off from the jet over the \nweekend. Ensembles continue to retrograde it off the Oregon coast\ninto the weekend. This will allow for the showery pattern to \ncontinue, with occasional embedded t-storm chances. Models are not\nentirely consistent on that detail but at this time Sunday has \nsome higher potential. Expect some moderating temperatures into \nthe weekend, though patchy frost may still be possible in \nsheltered valleys and outlying areas Saturday morning. /Solveig",
